Hi Dan,

The term Effective N has been used in a variety of ways. One primary way that it is used in the field is in the context of case-control studies. Oftentimes, the proportion of cases varies across studies being included in a meta-analysis. Because the proportion of cases can affect power, it is useful to obtain an "effective sample size" that represents the sample size for a theoretical case-control study with 50% of cases (a so-called "balanced design") that is equivalent in power to the case control study in question. In the Grotzinger preprint (https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.09.22.21263909), we show that it is the summation of these Effective Ns across samples composing a meta-analysis of case control traits that should be used for ldsc().

Mallard et al. used the term Effective N for another situation: one in which you have a set of summary statistics containing effect sizes and standard errors, but you do not know the sample size (e.g. it wasn't provided or the sumstats are for a GWAS of a factor). Using the effect sizes and standard errors, we can produce an "estimated sample size" associated with the OLS betas, or the "estimated effective sample size" associated with the logistic betas. We use the term N_hat to refer to estimating N when effect sizes are OLS regression coefficients, and EffN_hat when they are logistic regression coefficients. N_hat has the variance of the GWAS phenotype (usually 1) in the numerator, whereas EffN_hat has a 4 in the numerator. To avoid confusion we reserve the term "effective N" to refer to the expected sample size of a case-control study under a balanced design. Thus we can directly compute EffN from our knowledge of the number of cases and controls in each of the samples that contribute to a GWAS, or we can estimate it from the summary statistics.

I hope that helps to clarify things.

Elliot

